"""CPU-only clock passage/system reservation. No model inference."""
import hashlib,importlib.util,json,shutil
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;W=R.parents[1]
TEXTS=[
('discovery','canal','Explain how a canal lock moves a boat between water levels.',
'''A canal lock is a chamber with gates at both ends. Water can enter or leave the chamber through controlled openings, allowing its surface to rise or fall while a boat remains inside. The boat first waits for the chamber to reach the level of the water it is approaching from. One gate then opens, the boat enters slowly, and the gate closes behind it. If the boat needs to go uphill, water from the higher section fills the chamber. The rising water lifts the boat without requiring a crane or a powered platform. The far gate opens only after the levels on its two sides have become nearly equal. Going downhill uses the same sequence with water leaving instead of entering. Lock operators keep ropes secure without pulling them so tightly that they restrain the changing height of the vessel.'''),
('discovery','glaze','Explain what happens when a potter applies and fires a glaze.',
'''A glaze begins as a mixture of finely ground minerals suspended in water. The potter spreads this mixture over a piece of clay that has usually received an initial firing. The porous surface absorbs some water and leaves a thin mineral coating behind. Before the final firing, the base is cleaned so that the pot will not become stuck to the kiln shelf. As the temperature rises, some ingredients melt and combine with others to form a glassy layer. The final color depends on the minerals, the kiln atmosphere, and the temperature history. Cooling also matters because crystals can grow while the glaze passes through particular temperature ranges. A thick layer may flow down a wall while a thin layer can expose the underlying clay. Testing small samples helps the potter understand these effects before committing a larger vessel to the same firing schedule.'''),
('discovery','post','Describe how letters are sorted through a postal network.',
'''A letter enters a postal network when it is collected from a box or accepted at a counter. At a sorting center, machinery separates envelopes that can be handled automatically from items needing special attention. Printed routing information helps identify the destination region. Letters going to the same area are gathered into containers, which travel by road or other scheduled transport. At the receiving center, another sort divides them among local delivery routes. Workers then arrange the mail in an order that makes sense for each route. An unclear address can interrupt this process because the destination has to be checked manually. Packaging also affects handling, since a loose object or an uneven envelope can jam equipment. Throughout the network, containers and labels keep batches together while individual letters move between different stages of the sorting process.'''),
('discovery','loom','Explain the basic operation of a weaving loom.',
'''A loom holds one set of threads under tension so that another thread can pass between them. The stretched threads are called the warp, and the crossing thread forms the weft. A mechanism lifts some warp threads while leaving the others lower, creating an opening through which the weft travels. The new weft is then pressed against the previous rows. Changing which warp threads rise changes the pattern in the cloth. Keeping tension reasonably even is important because slack threads can become tangled or leave uneven spaces. The weaver must also watch the edges, where pulling the crossing thread too tightly can narrow the fabric. A simple pattern repeats a small lifting sequence, while more complicated equipment can control individual threads. As the cloth grows, finished fabric winds onto a beam and fresh warp feeds into the working area.'''),
('validation','marsh','Explain how a salt marsh changes with the tides.',
'''A salt marsh lies between land and coastal water, where the surface is flooded at some tides and exposed at others. Its plants must tolerate both salty water and periods of limited oxygen around their roots. Narrow channels carry water into the marsh as the tide rises and drain it as the tide falls. Sediment suspended in the water can settle among stems and leaves, gradually changing the height of the ground. Different elevations support different plants because some patches remain wet for longer than others. Small animals use this changing landscape for feeding and shelter. The edges of channels may shift as flowing water removes material from one place and deposits it somewhere else. These processes connect the daily movement of water with slower changes in the shape and vegetation of the marsh over many seasons.'''),
('validation','conveyor','Describe how a simple conveyor moves boxes through a warehouse.',
'''A conveyor moves boxes along a fixed path using a belt or a series of rollers. A motor provides the motion, while a supporting frame keeps the moving surface at a consistent height. Workers place boxes with enough separation that neighboring packages do not press against each other. Sensors can detect a box approaching a junction and tell a gate when to direct it toward another route. The required speed depends on the size of the boxes and the work performed at the next station. Increasing speed without considering that station can create a queue instead of improving the whole operation. A loose label or a damaged carton can catch on nearby equipment, so regular inspection matters. The conveyor is therefore one part of a coordinated handling process that also depends on packing, spacing, and receiving capacity.'''),
('validation','repair','Explain how a cobbler replaces a worn shoe sole.',
'''A cobbler starts by examining the upper part of the shoe and the way the old sole is attached. If the upper is badly damaged, replacing the sole may not be worthwhile. Otherwise the worn material is removed carefully so that the remaining structure can support a new layer. The cobbler cleans and prepares the attachment surfaces, then selects material with suitable thickness and flexibility. Some shoes need adhesive, some need stitching, and others use both. The replacement piece is trimmed to follow the shape of the shoe rather than leaving an awkward ledge around the edge. Finishing involves smoothing rough areas and checking that the shoe sits evenly on a flat surface. The goal is to restore a durable walking surface while preserving the fit and structure of the original upper.'''),
('validation','archive','Describe how a library preserves a collection of old maps.',
'''Old maps need storage that supports fragile paper without forcing it into tight folds. A librarian first records the condition of each item and notes tears, stains, or detached sections. Flat storage is often useful for large sheets, although the correct method depends on the material and its condition. Clean folders separate neighboring maps and make handling easier. Staff keep the room environment reasonably stable because rapid changes in moisture can cause paper to expand and contract. Light exposure is also limited to reduce fading of inks and pigments. When a reader requests a map, a large clear work surface helps prevent accidental creasing. Digital images can reduce repeated handling, but the original still needs care because its paper, markings, and physical details contain information that a scan may not fully capture.'''),
('validation','routes','Explain how a city map helps someone compare walking routes.',
'''A city map represents streets and landmarks at a scale smaller than the real place. To compare walking routes, a person first locates the starting point and the destination. The shortest line on the page is not always a usable route because buildings, rivers, and restricted areas can block direct travel. Street connections show where turns are possible, while crossings and bridges reveal how separate areas are linked. The scale gives an estimate of distance, but hills and busy junctions can make two equally long routes feel quite different. Landmarks help a walker check progress without constantly counting streets. A useful route can therefore balance distance with ease of navigation and available crossings. The map provides a structure for that decision, while observations on the ground supply details that may have changed since the map was made.'''),
('validation','insulation','Explain how insulation slows heat loss from a container.',
'''Insulation slows the transfer of heat between a container and its surroundings. Many insulating materials trap small pockets of air, limiting the movement of warmer and cooler regions through the material. Thickness matters because heat must pass through a longer path, but gaps can reduce the benefit of an otherwise thick layer. A lid is important too, since warm air and water vapor can escape through an open top. Shiny surfaces can reduce transfer by thermal radiation in suitable arrangements, although they do not replace the need to limit conduction and air movement. The rate of cooling also depends on the difference between inside and outside temperatures. Insulation does not create heat or prevent all transfer. It changes how quickly the container approaches the temperature of the environment around it.'''),
('validation','stage','Explain how a theater crew changes scenery between scenes.',
'''A theater crew prepares a scenery change by deciding which pieces move and where they must finish. Marks on the floor help workers place furniture and larger structures consistently. Some pieces travel on wheels, while others move along tracks or hang from overhead equipment. The sequence matters because one item may block the path of another if it moves too early. Lighting changes can direct the audience away from working areas, but the crew still needs a clear and practiced plan. Before a performance, rehearsals reveal places where a movement is awkward or a cue is difficult to hear. Small adjustments can save time without requiring everyone to rush. A successful change depends on predictable paths, secure attachments, and communication that lets each person know when the next movement can begin.'''),
('validation','orchard','Explain how an orchard worker thins fruit on a tree.',
'''An orchard worker thins fruit when a tree carries more developing fruit than its branches can support well. The worker removes selected small fruit while leaving enough space for those that remain to grow. Crowded clusters can limit size and increase rubbing between neighboring fruit. The choice of what to remove depends on the crop, branch strength, and the stage of growth. Damaged or unusually small fruit may be removed first, but the worker also considers how the remaining load is distributed across the tree. Thinning does not mean that every branch receives exactly the same number of fruit. It is an adjustment to the tree's shape and resources. Done at a suitable time, it can improve the final crop while reducing the chance that heavily loaded branches bend or break.''')]
